\title{A Fixed-Point Theory of Causal Consistency in Time Loops}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-13}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained, purely combinatorial-dynamical theory of time-travel
causal loops and the Novikov self-consistency principle. A \emph{causal loop} is
a finite cyclic chain of events in which the state of each event causally
determines the state of the next; a \emph{consistent history} is a state
assignment respecting all causal transitions and closing up into a genuine cycle.
Our central result is a structure theorem: for every causal loop, the consistent
histories stand in a canonical bijection with the fixed points of the loop's
\emph{round-trip map}---the composite of all its transition functions taken once
around. As an immediate corollary we obtain a precise form of the \emph{Novikov
self-consistency principle}: a loop admits a consistent history if and only if
its round-trip map has a fixed point, and the number of consistent histories
equals the number of fixed points. We show that the classical grandfather paradox
is the one-event loop whose transition is logical negation, and prove it has no
consistent history because negation has no fixed point. We then analyze repeated
traversal: going around a loop $k$ times realizes the $k$-th iterate of the
round-trip map, and on any finite non-empty state space a pigeonhole argument
guarantees that some positive number of repetitions is always consistent---even
for loops (like the grandfather) that are inconsistent on a single lap. The
theory reduces the entire logic of time-travel paradoxes to the existence and
counting of fixed points.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} Novikov self-consistency principle, causal loop,
fixed point, round-trip map, grandfather paradox, periodic point, pigeonhole
principle, discrete dynamical systems.

\section{Introduction}

The grandfather paradox---travel to the past, prevent your own existence, thereby
undoing the trip that made the prevention possible---has long served as the
canonical argument against time travel into the past. Igor Novikov's
\emph{self-consistency principle} offers an alternative to outright prohibition:
time travel may be geometrically permitted, but the only histories that
physically occur are those free of internal contradiction. Whatever happens must
be globally consistent with itself.

This paper asks a mathematical rather than a physical question: \textbf{given a
formalization of what a ``time loop'' and a ``consistent history'' are, exactly
when does a consistent history exist, and how many are there?} We answer this
completely. The answer is governed by a single classical notion---the fixed point
of a self-map---and the entire theory follows from elementary induction and the
pigeonhole principle. No topology, measure theory, or physics is required; the
development is finite and combinatorial.

\paragraph{Contributions.}
\begin{enumerate}
  \item A minimal, general definition of a \emph{causal loop} and of a
    \emph{consistent history} (Section~\ref{sec:defs}).
  \item The \emph{Structure Theorem} (Section~\ref{sec:structure}): consistent
    histories are in canonical bijection with fixed points of the round-trip map,
    together with the resulting cardinality identity.
  \item The \emph{Novikov Fixed-Point Criterion} (Section~\ref{sec:structure}):
    self-consistency is equivalent to the existence of a round-trip fixed point.
  \item A rigorous treatment of the \emph{grandfather paradox} as the negation
    loop, and a proof of its inconsistency (Section~\ref{sec:grandfather}).
  \item An analysis of \emph{repeated traversal} (Section~\ref{sec:repeat}): the
    iterate identity, and the theorem that on finite non-empty state spaces some
    repetition is always consistent.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Definitions}\label{sec:defs}

Throughout, $X$ denotes an arbitrary non-empty set of \emph{states}. We write
$k \bmod n$ for the least non-negative residue of $k$ modulo $n$.

\subsection{Causal loops}

\begin{definition}[Causal loop]
A \emph{causal loop} on state set $X$ consists of a positive integer $n$, the
\emph{length} (the number of events, with $n > 0$), and a family of
\emph{transition maps} $\step_i : X \to X$ for $i \in \N$, where $\step_i$
transports the state of event $i$ to the state of event $i+1$. Indices are read
cyclically: the causal link out of event $k$ is governed by $\step_{\,k \bmod n}$,
so the arrow out of the last event $e_{n-1}$ returns to $e_0$. We denote a loop by
$L = (n, \step)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Trajectory]
For a loop $L$ and a starting state $x \in X$, the \emph{trajectory}
$T_x : \N \to X$ is defined by recursion:
\[
T_x(0) = x, \qquad T_x(k+1) = \step_{\,k \bmod n}\bigl(T_x(k)\bigr).
\]
Thus $T_x(k)$ is the state produced after following $k$ causal arrows starting
from state $x$ at event $0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Round-trip map]
The \emph{round-trip map} of $L$ is $R : X \to X$, $R(x) = T_x(n)$, the state that
event $0$ returns to after the trajectory traverses the loop once. Explicitly,
\[
R(x) = \step_{n-1}\bigl(\step_{n-2}(\cdots \step_0(x)\cdots)\bigr).
\]
\end{definition}

\subsection{Consistent histories and self-consistency}

\begin{definition}[Consistent history]
A function $h : \N \to X$ is a \emph{consistent history} for $L$ if
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{(Causality)} $h(k+1) = \step_{\,k \bmod n}\bigl(h(k)\bigr)$ for
    all $k \in \N$; and
  \item \textbf{(Closure/periodicity)} $h(k + n) = h(k)$ for all $k \in \N$,
    i.e.\ $h$ is periodic with period $n$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Self-consistency]
The loop $L$ is \emph{self-consistent} if it admits at least one consistent
history. The \emph{Novikov self-consistency principle}, as a statement about a
given loop, is exactly the assertion that $L$ is self-consistent.
\end{definition}

The causality condition says the history obeys every causal arrow; the closure
condition says the history genuinely lives on the cycle (event $n$ \emph{is} event
$0$). A consistent history is thus a global, contradiction-free solution to the
loop.

\section{The Structure Theorem and the Novikov criterion}\label{sec:structure}

\begin{lemma}[Histories are trajectories]\label{lem:histtraj}
If $h$ satisfies the causality condition, then $T_{h(0)}(k) = h(k)$ for all $k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. For $k = 0$, $T_{h(0)}(0) = h(0)$. For the step,
$T_{h(0)}(k+1) = \step_{\,k \bmod n}(T_{h(0)}(k)) = \step_{\,k \bmod n}(h(k)) =
h(k+1)$, using the induction hypothesis and causality.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Fixed points unroll to periodic trajectories]\label{lem:fixperiodic}
If $R(x) = x$, then $T_x$ is periodic with period $n$: $T_x(k + n) = T_x(k)$ for
all $k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. For $k = 0$, $T_x(n) = R(x) = x = T_x(0)$. For the step, writing
$k + 1 + n = (k + n) + 1$,
\[
T_x(k+1+n) = \step_{\,(k+n) \bmod n}\bigl(T_x(k+n)\bigr)
           = \step_{\,k \bmod n}\bigl(T_x(k)\bigr) = T_x(k+1),
\]
using $(k+n) \bmod n = k \bmod n$ and the induction hypothesis.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Structure Theorem]\label{thm:structure}
For every causal loop $L$ there is a canonical bijection
\[
\{\, h : \N \to X \mid h \text{ is a consistent history} \,\}
\;\xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\;
\{\, x \in X \mid R(x) = x \,\}
\]
sending a history $h$ to its initial value $h(0)$, with inverse sending a fixed
point $x$ to its trajectory $T_x$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
\emph{Forward map.} Let $h$ be a consistent history. Then $h(0)$ is a fixed point
of $R$: by closure $h(n) = h(0)$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:histtraj} $T_{h(0)}(n) =
h(n)$; and $R(h(0)) = T_{h(0)}(n)$. Hence $R(h(0)) = h(n) = h(0)$.

\emph{Backward map.} Let $x$ be a fixed point of $R$ and set $h = T_x$. It
satisfies causality by definition and periodicity by
Lemma~\ref{lem:fixperiodic}, so it is a consistent history.

\emph{Mutual inversion.} From a history $h$, the backward map gives $T_{h(0)} = h$
by Lemma~\ref{lem:histtraj}. From a fixed point $x$, the forward map reads
$T_x(0) = x$. Both composites are the identity, so the maps are inverse
bijections.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Novikov Fixed-Point Criterion]\label{cor:novikov}
A causal loop $L$ is self-consistent if and only if its round-trip map $R$ has a
fixed point: $L \text{ self-consistent} \iff \exists\, x \in X,\; R(x) = x.$
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:structure}: the two sets are in bijection, so one
is non-empty iff the other is.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cardinality identity]\label{cor:card}
The number of consistent histories of $L$ equals the number of fixed points of
$R$: $\#\{\, h \mid h \text{ consistent} \,\} = \#\{\, x \mid R(x) = x \,\}.$
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A bijection preserves cardinality.
\end{proof}

\section{The grandfather paradox}\label{sec:grandfather}

\begin{definition}[Grandfather loop]
Let $X = \{\mathrm{true}, \mathrm{false}\}$ (interpret $\mathrm{true}$ as ``the
traveler is born''). The \emph{grandfather loop} $G$ has length $n = 1$ and single
transition $\step(b) = \lnot\, b$, logical negation.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:grandround}
The round-trip map of $G$ is negation: $R_G(b) = \lnot b$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Since $n = 1$, $R_G(b) = T_b(1) = \step_{\,0 \bmod 1}(b) = \lnot b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Grandfather paradox]\label{thm:grand}
The grandfather loop $G$ admits no consistent history; it is not self-consistent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Corollary~\ref{cor:novikov}, $G$ is self-consistent iff $R_G$ has a fixed
point, i.e.\ iff there is $b$ with $\lnot b = b$. But
$\lnot\,\mathrm{true} = \mathrm{false} \ne \mathrm{true}$ and
$\lnot\,\mathrm{false} = \mathrm{true} \ne \mathrm{false}$, so there is no such
$b$.
\end{proof}

By contrast, a loop whose transition is the identity has every state as a fixed
point (maximally consistent), and a loop with a constant transition $x \mapsto c$
has the unique fixed point $c$ (deterministic consistency).

\section{Repeated traversal}\label{sec:repeat}

\begin{lemma}[Shift identity]\label{lem:shift}
For all $x \in X$ and $m \in \N$, $\; T_x(n + m) = T_{R(x)}(m).$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $m$. For $m = 0$, $T_x(n) = R(x) = T_{R(x)}(0)$. For the step,
$T_x(n + m + 1) = \step_{\,(n+m) \bmod n}(T_x(n+m)) = \step_{\,m \bmod
n}(T_{R(x)}(m)) = T_{R(x)}(m+1)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Iterate identity]\label{thm:iterate}
For all $x$ and $k$, $\; R^{k}(x) = T_x(k\,n)$, where $R^{k}$ is the $k$-fold
composition of $R$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. For $k = 0$, $R^0(x) = x = T_x(0)$. For the step, $R^{k+1}(x) =
R^{k}(R(x)) = T_{R(x)}(kn) = T_x(n + kn) = T_x((k+1)n)$, using the induction
hypothesis and Lemma~\ref{lem:shift}.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[$k$-fold loop]
For $k > 0$, the \emph{$k$-fold loop} $L^{k}$ has length $kn$ and transitions
$\step^{(k)}_i = \step_{\,i \bmod n}$. It represents traversing $L$ a total of $k$
times before closing.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:powround}
The trajectory of $L^{k}$ agrees with that of $L$, and its round-trip map is the
$k$-th iterate of $R$: $\; T^{(k)}_x = T_x$ and $R_{L^{k}}(x) = R^{k}(x).$
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Trajectory agreement is an induction using $(j \bmod kn) \bmod n = j \bmod n$
(valid since $n \mid kn$). Then $R_{L^{k}}(x) = T^{(k)}_x(kn) = T_x(kn) = R^{k}(x)$
by Theorem~\ref{thm:iterate}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Periodic points on finite spaces]\label{lem:pigeon}
If $X$ is finite and non-empty, then every self-map $g : X \to X$ has a periodic
point: there exist $k > 0$ and $x \in X$ with $g^{k}(x) = x$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Fix $x_0 \in X$ and consider $x_0, g(x_0), g^2(x_0), \ldots$. Since $X$ is finite,
$m \mapsto g^{m}(x_0)$ is not injective on $\N$; by pigeonhole there are $i < j$
with $g^{i}(x_0) = g^{j}(x_0)$. Set $x = g^{i}(x_0)$ and $k = j - i > 0$; then
$g^{k}(x) = g^{j}(x_0) = g^{i}(x_0) = x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Consistency in the limit]\label{thm:limit}
Let $X$ be finite and non-empty. For every causal loop $L$ on $X$ there exists
$k > 0$ such that the $k$-fold loop $L^{k}$ is self-consistent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:pigeon} to $g = R$: obtain $k > 0$ and $x$ with $R^{k}(x) =
x$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:powround}, $R_{L^{k}}(x) = R^{k}(x) = x$, so by
Corollary~\ref{cor:novikov}, $L^{k}$ is self-consistent.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[Grandfather revisited]
For $G$, $R_G$ is negation: no fixed point (Theorem~\ref{thm:grand}) but
$R_G^2 = \mathrm{id}$, so $R_G^2$ fixes every state. Hence $G^2$ has two
consistent histories, the oscillations $(\mathrm{true},\mathrm{false},\ldots)$ and
$(\mathrm{false},\mathrm{true},\ldots)$. A single-lap paradox becomes a two-lap
consistent oscillation---Theorem~\ref{thm:limit} in its smallest instance.
\end{example}

\section{Applications and interpretation}

The framework offers a common language for the standard time-travel puzzles. The
\emph{grandfather paradox} is the negation loop (no fixed point, inconsistent).
The \emph{self-fulfilling loop} is any transition with a fixed point: the timeline
you create is the one you came from. The \emph{bootstrap paradox}---information
with no origin---is a nontrivial fixed point of an information-carrying
transition; consistency is fixedness, and the origin question dissolves. Finally,
\emph{multi-valued outcomes} are counted exactly by the number of fixed points
(Corollary~\ref{cor:card}), giving a quantitative measure of how under-determined
a loop is. The repeated-traversal results formalize that whether a loop is
``paradoxical'' can depend on the scale at which it closes.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The theory here is deliberately elementary---finite, combinatorial, and
measure-free---which is exactly what makes the Novikov principle provable rather
than merely postulated. Natural extensions include: (1) a \emph{continuous /
topological} Novikov principle over compact convex state spaces via the Brouwer or
Schauder fixed-point theorem; (2) \emph{probabilistic self-consistency}, where
transitions are Markov kernels and consistent histories become stationary
distributions of the round-trip kernel, always existing on finite spaces; (3)
\emph{counting and generic loops} via fixed-point statistics of random self-maps;
(4) \emph{uniqueness / determinism} conditions (e.g.\ contractions or unique
round-trip fixed points) for a deterministic time-travel regime; and (5)
\emph{interacting loops} sharing state, studied as a simultaneous fixed-point
problem connecting to Nash-style existence.

\section{Conclusion}

We have reduced the logic of time-travel causal loops to the theory of fixed
points. A consistent history is exactly a fixed point of the round-trip map
(Structure Theorem); the Novikov self-consistency principle is exactly the
existence of such a fixed point; the grandfather paradox is exactly the
fixed-point-free negation loop; and on finite state spaces, repeated traversal
always restores consistency by the pigeonhole principle. The mystery of
time-travel paradoxes, on this account, is the ancient and well-understood
mystery of whether a map fixes a point.

